[d38fa86953feac1a] (mini.pair-proposal.recovery.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.recovery`. The `recover_json_object` function is designed to prefer the last object inside a code fence if any fence holds one, otherwise the last top-level object in the text. If I provide a reply with a JSON object in a code fence followed by a JSON object in prose, the kernel should recover the one in the fence. If I rewrite the input to remove the code fence, the kernel should then recover the last top-level object (the one in prose). This tests the priority of fenced objects over top-level objects as described in the docstring.
commitments: {"kernel": "conformance.kernel.recovery", "input": "```json\n{\"a\": 1}\n```\n{\"b\": 2}", "rewritten": "{\"a\": 1}\n{\"b\": 2}", "expect": "moves", "rewrite": "Removed the code fence from the first object to change the recovery priority from fenced to last-top-level."}
[23260fa5af51f85b] (mini.pair-proposal.refusal.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.refusal-phrase`. The function `refusal_phrase_in` iterates through the `REFUSAL_PHRASES` list and returns the first phrase in that list that is found in the text, regardless of the order in which the phrases appear in the text itself. The list is `['I cannot', "I can't", "I'm sorry", 'I am unable', 'as an AI', 'cannot assist']`. If I provide an input containing both "I cannot" and "I'm sorry", the kernel will return "I cannot" because it appears first in the list. By rewriting the input to remove "I cannot", the kernel should then return "I'm sorry".
commitments: {"kernel": "conformance.kernel.refusal-phrase", "input": "I'm sorry, I cannot help.", "rewritten": "I'm sorry, I am unable to help.", "expect": "moves", "rewrite": "Replaced 'I cannot' (the highest priority phrase) with 'I am unable' (a lower priority phrase), allowing 'I'm sorry' to be the first match from the list."}
[9febb707ff09c786] (mini.pair-proposal.grounding.v1)
I am testing `conformance.kernel.grounding`. The `_grounding_kernel` function first checks if the span occurs in the document using `_span_occurs` with no relaxations. If the span is not found verbatim (after whitespace normalization), it returns `SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT`. In the input, the span is a verbatim match for the document, and the value is a case-insensitive match for the span, resulting in `GROUNDED`. By rewriting the span to include a character not present in the document, the `_span_occurs` check will fail, and the verdict should move to `SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT`.
